  "body": "### What does this PR do?\n\nBuilds QSA's bounded CANN attention mask by scattering integer zeros into an INT8 buffer, then converting to the required BOOL mask. On Ascend, boolean scatter can dominate this mask-construction path; the integer path preserves the same selected-token set, padding sentinel and output layout without changing attention semantics.\n\nThis is the complete successor to #1225, including its compact-index CANN forward/backward implementation because that foundation is not yet on `main`. The incremental performance change is confined to `_blocked_mask`: BOOL/False -> INT8/0, followed by `.bool()`.\n\n### Checklist Before Starting\n\n- Related: #1225 (bounded CANN QSA foundation).\n- Title follows the repository convention.\n\n### Test\n\n- Portable mask, fused-attention reference/autograd and KV-range checks: **3 passed, 1 skipped** (native Ascend test skipped on the CPU development host).\n- Exact mask equality covers duplicates, negative padding, entirely masked rows and non-contiguous indices. The CANN interface still receives a contiguous BOOL mask.\n- `make quality` with Ruff 0.13.2 and `git diff --check`: passed.\n- GPU unit CI already enumerates `tests/ops/`; this PR also registers the file in NPU unit CI. A guarded native test checks exact INT8 mask equality and CANN forward/backward against a CPU FP32 reference, including an entirely masked query chunk; its native result remains pending CI.\n- This PR does not claim that converting mask dtype universally produces a particular end-to-end speedup. Native CANN performance depends on attention shapes, selection density and distributed geometry.\n\n### API and Usage Example\n\nThe compact-index `qsa_npu_fused` interface remains as in #1225. Ulysses/CP communication stays outside this local operator. No training configuration change is required for the mask optimization.\n\n### Design & Code Changes\n\n- Bound temporary masks to the current query chunk and the selected KV interval.\n- Preserve the extra sentinel column for invalid indices; duplicate selections remain idempotent.\n- Retain fused softmax statistics for backward rather than replaying attention forward.\n- INT8 is temporary mask storage only; Q/K/V and attention arithmetic dtypes are unchanged.\n\n### Checklist Before Submitting\n\n- Read the contribution guide.\n- Formatting/lint and portable tests passed.\n- Tests included in GPU and NPU unit CI.\n- No task scripts, model data, runtime overlays or deployment configuration included.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **New Features**\n  - Added experimental Ascend NPU fused attention for Qwen4-Exp QSA.\n  - Supports compact selected-token indices, grouped-query attention, chunked processing, BF16 tensors, and gradient computation.\n  - Added input validation with clear errors for incompatible shapes, devices, dtypes, and indices.\n\n- **Documentation**\n  - Documented supported backends, constraints, tensor formats, chunking, masking, gradients, and unsupported fallback behavior.\n\n- **Tests**\n  - Added coverage for forward and backward correctness, masking edge cases, index handling, and Ascend-specific parity.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n### Maintainer repair — 2026-10-16\n\nNormally merged current upstream, retaining both lazy CUDA TileLang exports and guarded CANN QSA exports. Preserved upstream mHC and added QSA native CI gates. **3 portable checks passed, 1 native check skipped**. INT8 mask scatter and attention math are unchanged. Current-main package imports and exact mask/output/gradient CPU references are passing; native execution is a separate gate.\n\nPublished revision: `cd4767672a5c2b5b7fa44fc13e4da90d549a3fa0`. No force push or training JobRun modification.\n\nRegistry migration remains conditional on upstream #1142 landing. That breaking refactor is still open; these revisions target the current public-main dispatch API and do not claim compatibility with an unmerged future API.\n\n\n### Native NPU masked-row regression\n\nNative Ascend testing found that an all-masked query row can leave BF16 subnormal gradients (~1e-39) in the fused backend. Explicitly zero invalid-row outputs and upstream gradients; clear key/value gradients for a batch item with no valid query rows. Regression assertions remain exact-zero, without loosening tolerances.\n\nValidation: four portable QSA tests and the real-device QSA forward/backward parity test pass. A 32-rank Ulysses8/CP2 mixed GDN/QSA/PLE checkpointed small-model test has finite outputs and gradients, exact output parity, and zero cross-sample influence. Maximum relative L2 gradient differences versus the separately executed BF16 reference were 0.10% (input) and 0.67% (parameter). This is a scoped integration check; full-model throughput overhead of the added row guards has not yet been established.\n",
